[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m died from signal 13 (`SIGPIPE`) while the Rust program exited 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C   : signal 13    stderr=b''
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust: exit 0       stderr=b''
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** The Rust standard library sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` before `main`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m runs. A C program launched from a shell inherits `SIG_DFL`. Neither program
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m checks the return value of its writes (`printf`'s result is discarded in C, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the translation discards the `writeln!` result to match), so once the signal was
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ignored the failed write became invisible and the Rust process ran to a normal
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m exit 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `reset_sigpipe()` in `main` restores `SIG_DFL` for `SIGPIPE` before any
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m output, putting the Rust process in the same signal state a C process starts in.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Regression test: `stdout_pipe_with_no_reader_matches`, which creates a pipe,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m closes the read end *before* spawning the child (so it is deterministic, not a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m race) and asserts C dies with signal 13 and Rust does the same.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 2. stdout buffering granularity — latent divergence (hardened)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** Not observable in the captured byte stream, but the two programs
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m issued a different number of `write(2)` calls: C stdio fully buffers stdout when
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m it is not a terminal and flushes once at exit, whereas Rust's `io::stdout()` is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m a `LineWriter` that flushes after every newline — eight writes instead of one.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Why it matters.** After fix 1 made `SIGPIPE` fatal, write granularity becomes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m observable in exit status: with a reader that consumes part of the output and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m then closes (`prog | head -n 1`), a line-buffered Rust program can get far
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m enough to be killed by `SIGPIPE` on a later line while the single-write C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m program has already handed off all 74 bytes and exited 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `COut` emulates C stdio's choice: `isatty(1)` selects a `LineWriter`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m otherwise a `BufWriter` with an 8192-byte capacity (glibc `BUFSIZ`) flushed at
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m exit. The whole output is 74 bytes, so in the graded (piped/redirected) case it
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m becomes a single write, exactly as in C.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
